1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,440
Hi, in this video we'll show how you can use Burp to help test for parameter-based access control.

2
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:20,600
Some sites use insecure access controls based on request parameters. For example, a site might use

3
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:25,360
a role ID parameter to denote whether the user should be treated as having admin privileges

4
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,680
in the associated response. Even if these parameters don't enable you to bypass server-side access

5
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:38,640
controls, they might still be able to get past some client-side controls. For example, they might

6
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:44,240
reveal additional attack surface, such as an admin page that you can view but not necessarily interact

7
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:50,560
with. You can test the effect that these parameters have by using Burp Suite's match and replace rules

8
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:57,040
to swap out values and analyze the results. If you'd like to follow along, you can use the lab

9
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,680
User Role Controlled by Request parameter from our Web Security Academy.

10
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:07,440
Before you start, you'll need to map the application's visible attack surface.

11
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:14,560
We've already mapped out some of the lab here. Once you've done this, you can then go to the

12
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:21,920
top level menu and choose Burp Search. You can use the search bar to look for any potentially insecure

13
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:28,240
parameter values, such as false and read, and make a note of any values that return results.

14
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:36,000
In this application, although searching for read did not find anything, searching for false does return

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:46,960
results. So now go to the proxy tab and click proxy settings. We can configure a match and replace rule

16
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:53,200
to change the details as requests pass through Burp proxy. To configure a new match and replace rule,

17
00:01:54,160 --> 00:02:01,200
click add. Select request header from the type drop-down list. Enter one of the parameter values

18
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:08,320
that returned results during the search into the match field. So we'll add false. Now we can set the

19
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:17,280
value that should be used as the replacement, in this case true. Click OK to save your rule. You can

20
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:22,720
repeat these steps so the same replacement can be carried out in the request body and request param value

21
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:31,040
and for any additional parameter values. Now you can remap the site. As you remap the site,

22
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:50,320
look for signs that the attack surface has changed. For example, we can now see a link to the admin page.

